<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="problem-differentiability-and-lipschitz-continuity" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Differentiability and Lipschitz continuity
  </title>
  
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m> f \colon X \to \R </m> be a differentiable real-valued function defined on an interval <m> X \subseteq \R </m>. Prove that <m> f </m> is Lipschitz continuous if and only if its derivative <m> f' </m> is bounded.
    </p>
  </statement>
  
  <!-- <solution>
    <p>
      First suppose that <m> f </m> is Lipschitz continuous. Then there is some non-negative real number <m> L \geq 0 </m> so that
      
      <md>
        d \of{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } , f \of{ x_{ 2 } } } \leq L d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } }
      </md>
      
      for all points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X </m>. Let <m> c \in X </m>. Then for any point <m> x \in X \setminus \set{ c } </m>,
      
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \abs{ \frac{ f \of{ x } - f \of{ c } }{ x - c } } \amp = \frac{ \abs{ f \of{ x } - f \of{ c } } }{ \abs{ x - c } } = \frac{ d \of{ f \of{ x } , f \of{ c } } }{ d \of{ x , c } }
        </mrow>
        
        <mrow>
          \amp \leq \frac{ L d \of{ x , c } }{ d \of{ x , c } } = L
        </mrow>
      </md>,
      
      and so <m> -L \leq \frac{ f \of{ x } - f \of{ c } }{ x - c } \leq L </m>. <xref ref="proposition-comparison-of-limits"/> implies that <m> -L \leq f' \of{ c } \leq L </m>. Since this holds for all <m> c \in X </m>, we conclude that <m> f' </m> is bounded.
    </p>
    
    <p>
      Conversely, now suppose that <m> f' </m> is bounded. Then there is a non-negative real number <m> L \geq 0 </m> so that <m> -L \leq f' \of{ x } \leq L </m> for all points <m> x \in X </m>. We will show that <m> d \of{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } , f \of{ x_{ 2 } } } \leq L d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } </m> for all points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X </m>. Indeed, suppose for a contradiction that <m> d \of{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } , f \of{ x_{ 2 } } } \gt L d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } </m> for some points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X </m>. Clearly <m> x_{ 1 } \neq x_{ 2 } </m>, or both <m> d \of{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } , f \of{ x_{ 2 } } } </m> and <m> d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } </m> would be <m> 0 </m>. Without loss of generality <m> x_{ 1 } \gt x_{ 2 } </m>.
    </p>
    
    <p>
      Since <m> f </m> is continuous on <m> \clint{ x_{ 1 } }{ x_{ 2 } } </m> and differentiable on <m> \opint{ x_{ 1 } }{ x_{ 2 } } </m>, the <xref ref="theorem-mean-value-theorem" text="title"/> implies that
      
      <md>
        f' \of{ c } = \frac{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } - f \of{ x_{ 2 } } }{ x_{ 1 } - x_{ 2 } }
      </md>
      
      for some interior point <m> c \in \opint{ x_{ 1 } }{ x_{ 2 } } </m>. We observe that
      
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \abs{ f' \of{ c } } \amp = \abs{ \frac{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } - f \of{ x_{ 2 } } }{ x_{ 1 } - x_{ 2 } } } = \frac{ \abs{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } - f \of{ x_{ 2 } } } }{ \abs{ x_{ 1 } - x_{ 2 } } } 
        </mrow>
        
        <mrow>
          \amp = \frac{ d \of{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } , f \of{ x_{ 2 } } } }{ d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } } \gt \frac{ L d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } }{ d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } } = L
        </mrow>
      </md>.
      
      This contradiction implies that <m> d \of{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } , f \of{ x_{ 2 } } } \leq L d \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } </m> for all points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X </m>; that is, <m> f </m> is Lipschitz continuous.
    </p>
  </solution> -->
</exercise>
